From: Lachlan Green <69705346+CL0utM4N@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dts: dlink-dsr-1000n: switch port 5 <-> eth1 as "wanlink"

Switch port 5 is wired to the Octeon's second RGMII port (eth1); D-Link's
firmware used it as the WAN path (port-based VLAN WAN1 + port 5). Describe
it as a user port with the same fixed RGMII link as the CPU port, so it can
be bridged with "wan" in the switch and WAN traffic gets its own untagged
link into the CPU.

diff -Nurp a/arch/mips/boot/dts/cavium-octeon/dlink_dsr-1000n.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/cavium-octeon/dlink_dsr-1000n.dts
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/cavium-octeon/dlink_dsr-1000n.dts	2026-11-11 22:00:03.248122961 +1100
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/cavium-octeon/dlink_dsr-1000n.dts	2026-11-11 22:00:03.290143639 +1100
@@ -94,6 +94,22 @@
 				reg = <4>;
 				label = "wan";
 			};
+			/*
+			 * Port 5 is wired to the Octeon's second RGMII port (eth1),
+			 * which D-Link used as the WAN path. As a user port it can be
+			 * bridged with "wan" in the switch, giving WAN traffic its
+			 * own untagged link into the CPU.
+			 */
+			port@5 {
+				reg = <5>;
+				label = "wanlink";
+				phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
+
+				fixed-link {
+					speed = <1000>;
+					full-duplex;
+				};
+			};
 			port@8 {
 				reg = <8>;
 				ethernet = <&{/soc@0/pip@11800a0000000/interface@0/ethernet@0}>;
